<!--
  RESEARCH CONSENT GATE

  The deployed route "/" lands here, not on the course. Three states, one
  Vertical: unanswered → the IRB form; answered but not acknowledged → a short
  verdict-neutral acknowledgment with the course behind one Continue;
  acknowledged → the course itself. Gates test ANSWERED (any value), never
  ACCEPTED: declining changes nothing about the course, including this gate.

  The form block is also a child of wj_course (nav item "Research Consent"),
  same block, same state — so "you may leave the study at any time" is a page
  students can actually revisit, and a later change of answer never locks
  anything (both values are truthy).

  IRB language: University of Memphis, same protocol/grant as the psychology
  SBAs (source: edu.memphis.psych psychology/consent.olx). Adapted for ENGL
  1010: (a) "obtained from Canvas" dropped — this course's work lives on this
  platform; (b) psych's instructor sentence (Dr. Anderson) generalized to
  "your instructor"; (c) the psych co-investigators removed and the contact
  is Professor Fredlund, the head instructor — Floyd/Anderson are psychology
  people with no role here (Piotr). PI (Dr. Sabatini) unchanged — same
  protocol. FLAG: confirm the adapted text against the approved protocol
  before launch.

  FORM VERSION: wj-consent-2026-08-30-a (constant at the bottom of the form).
  If the language changes, bump it and plan re-consent. The version is not
  stamped per-student: to know which text a consent was given under, correlate
  the consent event's timestamp with the deploy history of this file.
-->

<Vertical id="wj_study" title="Writing Journals" launchable="true">

<!-- HACK: when= directly on these <Use> tags rendered unconditionally, so the
     gating lives on wrapper Verticals. Per Piotr, <Use> supports overrides and
     when= SHOULD work — this is a platform bug (bugs.md); unwrap when fixed. -->
<Vertical id="wj_irb_form_gate" when="!@wj_irb_consent.value">
<Use ref="wj_irb_consent_form" />
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="wj_irb_ack" when="@wj_irb_consent.value && !@wj_irb_ack_done.value">
<Markdown when="@wj_irb_consent.value === 'consented'">
## Thank you

Your course work may be included in the research, always de-identified. You
can change your answer at any time under **Research Consent** in the course
navigation.
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@wj_irb_consent.value === 'declined'">
## No problem

Your course work stays out of the research. Nothing about the course changes —
same activities, same credit. You can change your answer at any time under
**Research Consent** in the course navigation.
</Markdown>
<Done id="wj_irb_ack_done" label="Continue to the Writing Journals" />
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="wj_course_gate" when="@wj_irb_consent.value && @wj_irb_ack_done.value">
<Use ref="wj_course" />
</Vertical>

<!-- The form definition, parked unrendered (the <Use> above and the course
     nav render it). Same pattern as wj1's card deck. -->
<Hidden id="wj_consent_defs">
<Vertical id="wj_irb_consent_form" title="Research Consent">

<TalkBubble who="Katie Fredlund" cast="consent.cast">
<Markdown>
As a college instructor, I am always working to improve my courses so
that my students are successful.

As part of this goal, I conduct classroom-based studies. This involves
asking my students to do different activities that give me information
about them or involves me testing out ideas. I collect this
information to be a better instructor. However, I learn about ways to
help other college instructors support their students. When this
happens, I want to be able to share what I have learned from
de-identified student course work.

For me to use the information that I gather from students in this way,
I need their consent. Below is an informed consent form for this
class. Please read the form. Whichever way you answer, the course — your
activities, your grade, your experience — is exactly the same.
</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Markdown>
# University of Memphis

## Informed Consent

**Title:** Scenario-Based Assessment in the age of generative AI: Making space in the education market for alternative assessment paradigms

**Principal Investigator:** Dr. John Sabatini

**Sponsor:** Institute for Intelligent Systems (IIS)

## Procedures

You are being asked to take part in a research study. If you decide to
take part, you will agree to share some of your course work with the
researchers. This work includes writing assignments, online
discussions, and assessments you produce for the course. These
materials will be obtained after grades are submitted and
will be accessed only by the researchers.

No work beyond that which you would normally do as part of this course
will be required. The research will require no more time than you
would normally spend on the course. The decision to participate, or
not to take part, will have no effect on your grade or standing in
this class.

Your instructor will not know whether you consented to this research
until after they submit the grades for the course. You will not
interact with any of the other researchers.

## Voluntary Participation and Withdrawal

You do not have to be in this study, and you may choose to leave the study at any time.

## Contact Information

Contact Professor Katherine Fredlund at kfrdlund@memphis.edu.

## Consent

Please choose one. Either answer takes you into the course.
</Markdown>

<ChoiceInput id="wj_irb_consent">
  <Key value="consented"> I accept. </Key>
  <Key value="declined" > I do not want to participate in this study. </Key>
</ChoiceInput>

<!-- Consent-form text version; analytics joins consent values against this.
     Bump on ANY change to the language above. -->
<Markdown id="wj_irb_consent_version" when="false">wj-consent-2026-08-30-a</Markdown>

</Vertical> <!-- wj_irb_consent_form -->
</Hidden>
</Vertical> <!-- wj_study -->
